Username Enumeration via Response Difference + Brute Force
🎯 Objective

Identify a valid username using response differences, then brute-force the password despite login protections.

🧠 Vulnerability

The application leaks information through inconsistent error messages:

Invalid username → generic error
Valid username → different response after multiple attempts

This creates a username enumeration vulnerability, which can be chained with brute-force.

🔍 Initial Observation

I started by testing the login with random credentials:

username=invalid-user&password=wrongpass

Then intercepted the request using Burp and sent it to Intruder.

🧪 Phase 1: Username Enumeration
⚙️ Attack Setup
Attack type → Cluster Bomb
Payload positions:
username=§invalid-username§&password=example§§
🧠 Why this setup?
First payload → list of usernames
Second payload → null payload (5 times)

👉 This repeats each username multiple times to trigger rate-limit behavior

🚀 Attack Execution
Loaded username wordlist
Set second payload → Null payload → 5 iterations
Started attack
📊 Key Finding

Most responses were similar.

But for one username:

Response length increased
Error message changed to:
"You have made too many incorrect login attempts"

👉 This confirms:

Username is valid
Backend logic behaves differently for real users
🧪 Phase 2: Password Brute Force

Now I switched strategy.

⚙️ Attack Setup
Attack type → Sniper
Fixed username = discovered valid username
Payload position:
username=valid-user&password=§password§
🔍 Enhancement (Important)

Added Grep Extract rule:

Extract error message from response

👉 This helps detect anomalies automatically

🚀 Attack Execution
Loaded password list
Started attack
📊 Key Finding

Observed:

Most responses → error message present
One response → NO error message

👉 That means:

Login succeeded
Server didn’t return failure message
⏳ Rate Limit Handling

After brute-force:

Waited ~1 minute for lock reset

👉 Important because:

Account gets temporarily blocked after failed attempts
🔓 Final Step

Used discovered credentials:

username: valid-user
password: identified-password

Successfully logged in and accessed account page.

💥 Key Learning
Always look for response differences, not just status codes
Rate-limiting can actually help detect valid users
Repeating payloads (null payload trick) is extremely useful
“No error message” is often a success indicator
🧠 My Thought Process
Tried random login → observed generic errors
Thought → “What if valid user behaves differently?”
Triggered rate limit intentionally
Detected anomaly → extracted valid username
Switched to focused brute-force
Used grep → found success signal
🛡️ Fix / Prevention
Use consistent error messages
Avoid revealing rate-limit differences
Implement:
IP-based + account-based throttling
CAPTCHA after failures
Monitor abnormal login attempts
